{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:labor:audit","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:3e7fdf1cb83d831e12acbd83180a86fd365f6dd3be98bef8ffbe2485b7ecdf0f","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.13,"B":0.49,"C":0.3,"D":0.08}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"LOCK CONFIRMED AND AUDITED. I applied lexical priority strictly: (1) A if CQ3 growth >= 2.6; (2) else C if OECD-38 GDP-weighted core 2032-36 >= 2.7 AND growth < 2.0; (3) else D if growth < 1.7 AND core < 2.0; (4) else B. The rule is well-formed and resolves every overlap and every gap, with one consequence the literal definitions hide: rule 3 requires core < 2.0 on the SAME PPP-GDP-weighted OECD-38 measure that CQ2 uses, and that measure carries a ~+0.4 to +0.5pp Türkiye-plus-LatAm floor over 2032-36 (see cq2_reasoning). A genuine BIS-style demand-shortfall world with underlying G7 core at ~1.5% still PRINTS ~2.0-2.1% on this aggregate. Regime D is therefore substantially harder to resolve YES than its economic description suggests — the artefact drains D into C and B. That is my largest MECE finding.\n\nDERIVATION FROM MY OWN CQ3/CQ2.\n- A: from my CQ3 (P50 1.9, P90 2.8, roughly lognormal-ish right tail), P(growth >= 2.6) = 0.13. A = 13. Consistency check passed: P50 1.9 forbids putting most mass on A, and I have not.\n- Remaining 87. P(growth < 2.0) = 0.55 of total (P50 1.9 sits just below 2.0).\n- Within the 55 points of growth < 2.0, split on core inflation: P(core >= 2.7 | growth < 2.0) = 0.55 — my CQ2 conjunct-A marginal is 0.86 for >= 2.50% over 2027-36, and 2.7% over 2032-36 is ~0.58 unconditionally (Türkiye disinflating partially by the 2030s shrinks but does not remove the floor), shaded slightly down because part of the low-growth mass is the deflationary-bust branch where the artefact itself fades. C = 55 x 0.55 = 30.\n- P(core < 2.0 | growth < 2.0) = 0.14 given the artefact floor. D = 55 x 0.14 = 7.7 -> 8.\n- Low-growth / moderate-inflation leftover (growth < 2.0, core 2.0-2.69) falls to B by rule 4: 55 - 30 - 7.7 = 17.3.\n- Growth 2.0-2.59 mass = 87 - 55 = 32, all B.\n- B = 32 + 17.3 = 49.3 -> 49. Sum = 13 + 49 + 30 + 8 = 100.\n\nRESIDUAL-B ABSORPTION, QUANTIFIED. B's own literal band (growth 1.7-2.5%) accounts for ~32pp. The rule-4 catch-all adds ~17pp from the growth-below-2.0-with-core-between-2.0-and-2.7 cell, which a naive reader of the prose labels would have split between C (\"low growth, term-premium spikes\") and D (\"low growth, excess capacity\"). So B is inflated by ~17pp, ~35% of its own mass, and a naive reading would have produced roughly C 38 / D 15 / B 34.\n\nONE SUPPORTING LEG OF C THAT I DO NOT ACCEPT. C's \"grid supply wall\" is not supported by AI-Q3 arithmetic at P50: 365 -> 998 TWh is +633 TWh over 12 years, ~72 GW of average load, ~145-180 GW firm-equivalent or ~250-350 GW if VRE-plus-storage, i.e. ~$400-900B of generation and grid capex, ~$35-75B/yr against OECD-38+China power-sector capex of ~$600-800B/yr: 5-10%, not a wall. Even at P90 (1,574 TWh) it is 10-18%. The identity closes; the binding constraint is interconnection QUEUE TIME, not capital, which is a siting constraint no capex series will show. My C = 30 therefore rests on the inflation-measurement leg, not the grid leg.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:51.354Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"TÜİK publishes Türkiye calendar-2029 core CPI (ex food and energy, annual average) below 8.0%, and OECD.Stat's OECD-total core aggregate narrows to within 0.25pp of the G7 core aggregate.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31 2030 (Jan-2030 print plus OECD aggregate revision)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes ~0.35pp of